{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:52:46Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 226, "kind": "message", "text": "Design review complete. Summary:\n\n**What I found:** `docs/design.md`'s contrast math had real errors \u2014 most notably `#16191c`'s luminance was off by ~10x (0.096 stated vs. 0.0095 actual), and `#0066cc`/`#a8480a` were overstated by ~2.6x. That caused the doc to wrongly conclude the dark-orange background failed the 3:1 minimum and to rationalize shipping that shortfall anyway (\"established pattern in accessible design systems\") rather than fixing it \u2014 which is exactly backwards from what a design review should do. With correct math, every combination actually clears 3:1 with real margin; no CSS values needed to change.\n\nSeparately, `docs/implementation.md` documents a deviation the developer made (inset white outline instead of outset box-shadow for `.filter-option.active`/`.priority-select.priority-high`, to avoid the ring being clipped/occluded on flush-adjacent filter buttons) that was never folded back into `docs/design.md` \u2014 the design doc still only described the single global rule while the shipped `frontend/src/App.css:153-173` has two rules. The developer's geometric reasoning was sound, so I adopted it into the design record and computed contrast for it (17.65:1 and 5.84:1 \u2014 both pass comfortably).\n\n**What changed:** Only `docs/design.md` \u2014 corrected contrast tables, added the \"Override for flush/dark-fill controls\" section, updated the CSS snippets and traceability table to match what's actually implemented. No code changes; `frontend/src/App.css` already matches the (now-accurate) design.\n\n**Handoff:** Design is now internally consistent with the implementation and accessibility-verified. Ready for the reviewer stage (test pass + review against `docs/spec.md`'s 7 ACs) \u2014 no further design or development work needed for this cycle.", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "79e2ac9d-8f8f-4122-ac4c-30abb4aa5ed1"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:52:46Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 227, "kind": "status", "text": "Design review complete. Summary:\n\n**What I found:** `docs/design.md`'s contrast math had real errors \u2014 most notably `#16191c`'s luminance was off by ~10x (0.096 stated vs. 0.0095 actual), and `#0066cc`/`#a8480a` were overstated by ~2.6x. That caused the doc to wrongly conclude the dark-orange background failed the 3:1 minimum and to rationalize shipping that shortfall anyway (\"established pattern in accessible design systems\") rather than fixing it \u2014 which is exactly backwards from what a design review should do. With correct math, every combination actually clears 3:1 with real margin; no CSS values needed to change.\n\nSeparately, `docs/implementation.md` documents a deviation the developer made (inset white outline instead of outset box-shadow for `.filter-option.active`/`.priority-select.priority-high`, to avoid the ring being clipped/occluded on flush-adjacent filter buttons) that was never folded back into `docs/design.md` \u2014 the design doc still only described the single global rule while the shipped `frontend/src/App.css:153-173` has two rules. The developer's geometric reasoning was sound, so I adopted it into the design record and computed contrast for it (17.65:1 and 5.84:1 \u2014 both pass comfortably).\n\n**What changed:** Only `docs/design.md` \u2014 corrected contrast tables, added the \"Override for flush/dark-fill controls\" section, updated the CSS snippets and traceability table to match what's actually implemented. No code changes; `frontend/src/App.css` already matches the (now-accurate) design.\n\n**Handoff:** Design is now internally consistent with the implementation and accessibility-verified. Ready for the reviewer stage (test pass + review against `docs/spec.md`'s 7 ACs) \u2014 no further design or development work needed for this cycle.", "meta": {"native_type": "result", "subtype": "success", "session_id": "79e2ac9d-8f8f-4122-ac4c-3